# Module 1: Introduction to SELinux

## Overview

Security-Enhanced Linux (SELinux) is a Mandatory Access Control (MAC) framework built into the Linux kernel. Unlike traditional Linux permissions, SELinux applies security policies that determine which users, processes, and applications can access specific system resources.

SELinux is commonly deployed in enterprise environments where servers contain confidential information or run business-critical applications.

### Topics Covered

* Introduction to SELinux
* Mandatory Access Control (MAC)
* Difference between DAC and MAC
* Linux security architecture
* SELinux components
* Resource labeling
* Security policies
* Security contexts
* Confined vs Unconfined users
* Enterprise use cases

