# Module 6: Managing SELinux Users

Linux users must be mapped to SELinux users before SELinux policies can control their access.

### SELinux User Types

* unconfined_u
* staff_u
* user_u
* guest_u

### Commands

View SELinux users

```bash
sudo semanage user -l
```

View login mappings

```bash
sudo semanage login -l
```

Create mapping

```bash
sudo semanage login -a \
-s staff_u username
```

Delete mapping

```bash
sudo semanage login --delete \
-s staff_u username
```

### Practical Exercise

* Map Linux users to SELinux users.
* Verify mappings.
* Test SSH login.
* Remove mappings.
* Compare confined and unconfined users.

